FPAC 4.1.6-rc7 — node records updated again between nodes (release candidate)
=============================================================================

  * The White Pages record of a node is updated again by its
    neighbours. fpacwpd did not recognise the other nodes as WP
    servers and refused their updates of node records; an unrefreshed
    record ended up marked deleted, and "C CALLSIGN" to that node
    failed for lack of a White Pages entry.

  * A changed record is relayed at once to the other neighbours, and
    only one White Pages session is kept with each neighbour.

  * White Pages exchanges between two nodes are bounded: at most
    three vector rounds in a row per hour with a neighbour, and two
    versions of a record bearing the same date can no longer replace
    each other endlessly between nodes.

  * The benefit is complete once both ends of a link run this
    version: a node still on an older version keeps refusing node
    record updates from its neighbours.

  * The "Routes" command lists the ROSE routes by increasing address:
    each DNIC starts with its DNIC-wide route, followed by its
    addresses in increasing order.

  * The headings of the "Nodes" and "Users" commands now say what is
    listed: the callsigns to connect to for "Nodes"; the AX.25 links
    (users and links between nodes), then the ROSE circuits, for
    "Users". Their help texts are rewritten.

  * New "DNic" command (DN): the countries of the DNICs this node
    has routes for; "DN 5050" names the country of any DNIC.

FPAC 4.1.6-rc6 — no more unreadable callsign when fpad stops (release candidate)
================================================================================

  * When FPAC is stopped or restarted, the node no longer sends frames
    with an unreadable source callsign (")0=|U}-10", "*") and no longer
    opens ghost AX.25 links to its neighbours: fpad was overwriting the
    kernel's ROSE callsign instead of reading it.

FPAC 4.1.6-rc5 — complete output through two nodes, ROSE before NetRom (release candidate)
==========================================================================================

  * A long output seen through two nodes (for example "u" on a remote
    node) no longer arrives truncated or without its prompt: fpacnode
    acting as a gateway was dropping blocks when the link to the user
    was full.

  * "C CALLSIGN" to an FPAC node known to the White Pages goes out over
    ROSE, even if a NetRom node has the same callsign. The NetRom alias
    remains the way to ask for NetRom.

  * The message about nearby NetRom nodes is no longer displayed before
    a successful ROSE connection.

FPAC 4.1.6-rc4 — callsigns without SSID and exact NetRom connection (release candidate)
=======================================================================================

  * A callsign without an SSID is now everywhere the same as the one
    with SSID 0 (F3KT = F3KT-0): connection to heard stations with
    SSID 0, applications and aliases declared without an SSID, ROSE
    failover.

  * "C CALLSIGN" no longer goes to another NetRom node whose callsign
    starts the same way (c f6bvp-1 went to F6BVP-12). A message lists
    the nearby NetRom nodes when there is no exact match.

FPAC 4.1.6-rc3 — usable routes in the "r" command (release candidate)
=====================================================================

  * The "r" command now also displays the exact routes to adjacent
    nodes, which were hidden.

  * "r <address>" shows the route actually used to connect to that
    address, and no longer only the general routes.

  * The "Relaying via ..." message of the ROSE connection and the
    failover after a failure now follow the kernel's route order: they
    sometimes named the neighbour of the general DNIC route instead of
    the neighbour of the specific route actually used.

  * "r" displays, in a "Connect" column, the callsign to use to connect
    to each adjacent node, and "r <address>" shows the connect command.
    The "n" command now titles its callsigns "Connect".

  * fpacwpd no longer spins at 100 % CPU (White Pages blocked) when a
    client disconnects in the middle of a reply, and a synchronisation
    neighbour no longer stays stuck in "connection in progress" (White
    Pages synchronisation stopped with that node), nor does fpacwpd
    crash after a synchronisation connection is closed.

  * The "w" command displays all the White Pages records, most recent
    first, page by page (<Return> to continue, A to abort).

  * White Pages: a node record is no longer moved by connections made
    under the same callsign (the F6BVP-0 record changed address
    depending on the node where the connection took place).

FPAC 4.1.6-rc2 — port callsigns in the "Ports" command (release candidate)
==========================================================================

  * The fpacnode "Ports" command displays a new "Callsign" column: the
    callsign of each AX.25 and NetRom port, and the ROSE address of the
    rose0 port. The columns stay aligned with their titles whatever the
    terminal.

  * The "u" command now displays the real port of each link: netnod
    for NetRom links, rose0 for ROSE links, and no longer axudp for all
    of them.

FPAC 4.1.6-rc1 — ROSE connection fixes (release candidate)
==========================================================

  * The "Relaying via ..." message of the connect command now names the
    neighbour actually used by the kernel: first an "Opened" neighbour
    (ROSE link established), and a "Closed" neighbour only as a last
    resort. The automatic failover also removes the route of the right
    neighbour after a failure.

  * NetRom connection: the connection message now shows the neighbour
    actually used ("NetRom relaying via ..." or "direct"), with the
    quality and the number of the route, and reports when what was
    typed designates a different node than expected.

  * NetRom node list: the colour shows whether the node is really
    reachable (green: quality above 200 and neighbour without failure;
    yellow: the rest; red: quality 0 or 120, invalid active route,
    failed neighbour or AX.25 link awaiting connection), in all three
    sort orders and in "ne *". A connection to a node whose active
    route is invalid now displays a warning instead of staying without
    an answer.

  * Message language: the command prompt and the connection and routing
    messages are displayed in English or in French, according to the
    system language (or the "Language = en | fr | auto" line of
    fpac.conf). The "LAng 1" (English) or "LAng 2" (French) command
    changes the language of the current session.

  * wpedit: confirmation messages are visible immediately; deleting a
    record that is already deleted (or restoring a record that is not
    deleted) no longer ends the session.


FPAC 4.1.5 — reliable White Pages, clearer connections
======================================================

This release gathers all the work accumulated since 4.1.4, around
three main themes: the reliability of the White Pages, the clarity of
the connection messages, and a few display improvements.

WHITE PAGES (network directory)

  * A record marked "deleted" by the nightly cleanup no longer stays
    stuck: it can again repair itself as soon as the originating node
    re-announces its address, without manual intervention.

  * Each node now re-announces its own record every 7 days, even
    without a restart, so that it never looks stale to the other nodes
    of the network.

  * The delay before a deleted record is permanently erased is now
    correctly respected (it used to collapse to about one day).

  * A record marked "deleted" no longer appears in the user Wp command
    (it remains visible to the sysop through wplist), which avoids
    attempting connections to obsolete addresses.

  * A successful explicit ROSE connection to a callsign whose record
    was marked "deleted" now restores it automatically.

  * The ROSE failover is more robust: it also considers a neighbour
    whose link is not up yet, and the messages displayed now name the
    neighbour actually tried instead of staying vague.

CLEARER CONNECTIONS

  * The "Trying (user port) CALLSIGN..." message now gives the name of
    the port used, directly inside the parentheses (e.g. "Trying (user
    port AXUDP) CALLSIGN...") — handy to know which way a connection
    goes out, including when it is resolved through the list of heard
    stations (mheard).

  * A short ROSE address typed without the DNIC was silently completed
    with the DNIC of the local node instead of the correspondent's,
    which could send the connection to the wrong network without any
    notice. A warning is now displayed immediately.

  * "Connect CALLSIGN via DIGIPEATER" (without giving a port) was no
    longer interpreted correctly and failed or lost the requested
    digipeater. Fixed.

  * Connecting to an alias now displays what it really corresponds to,
    and a message says which ROSE relay is being tried, even for an
    address typed explicitly.

DISPLAY

  * Fixed a misalignment in the AX.25 state column of the users list
    ("u" command), and the display of the "Disc pending" state.

  * In the NetRom node list ("ne" command), a quality still frozen at
    its startup value (120) is now displayed in red instead of green,
    to spot at a glance the entries never really confirmed by the
    network.

  * The node status ("s") clearly distinguishes the declared user port
    from the port actually used by the current session, and displays
    the exact build time.

  * The fpacnode display colours can be customised without recompiling
    through /usr/local/etc/ax25/fpacnode.colors (see that file for the
    details of the available settings).

CHANGELOG.fr AND NOUVELLES

  * The ChangeLog and this NEWS file are now also available in French
    (ChangeLog.fr and NOUVELLES).

This release also fixes several crashes and build errors inherited
from previous versions (see ChangeLog for the full history).

